<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enderun School</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Enderûn Mektebi, Enderun Academy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Enderun School (Enderûn Mektebi) was a palace and boarding school in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapi Palace</a>, run by the Inner Service (Enderûn) of the Ottoman palace, chiefly for princes of the court and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>. Its students were mostly recruited through the devşirme, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamization">Islamization</a> of abducted Christian slave children for service in the Ottoman government and army. Mehmet II improved the palace school founded by his father, Murat II, to select the best youths of the empire and train them for government. Three preparatory schools outside the palace held some 1,000–2,000 students and the palace school about 300, who studied Islamic and positive sciences, languages, law, music and arms.</p></div>
